    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "837beecf-4239-4b3d-b683-1d611af3da2e",
      "key": "7a896023-ffec-4498-ad46-b9fbd8a6f18c",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "featurePropertyFeatureTitle",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": "Itinerary"
        },
        {
          "alias": "richTextContent",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": {
              "contentData": [],
              "settingsData": [],
              "expose": [],
              "layout": {}
            },
            "markup": "\u003Ch3\u003EDay 1\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWe meet our tour manager at our designated pick up points and transfer by private coach to London St Pancras station for our Eurostar journey to Brussels in second class coaches. On arrival in Brussels we are met by our executive coach and travel to the Horta Museum. Our guided tour on arrival introduces us to the house and studio once owned by Victor Horta, the architect who changed the face of the city. We continue to the centrally located 4* Novotel Hotel for a five night stay. A welcome dinner is included in our hotel this evening.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 2\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOur morning guided walking tour of Antwerp takes in sights such as the Great Market Square, Town Hall, Butchers\u2019 Guild House and the Cathedral of Our Lady. This afternoon we enjoy a guided tour at DIVA, the Museum of Jewellery, Silver and Diamonds. Located in Antwerp, the diamond capital of the world, we discover how diamonds are mined, how silversmiths work, and learn about the art and use of these beautiful objects. The museum is home to a collection of 650 diamonds, pieces of jewellery, and silver objects dating from the 15th century to the present day.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 3\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOur morning guided coach tour of Brussels takes in the city\u2019s main districts including the Grand Place, the Royal District and the Louise District. We then enjoy a guided tour of the Museum of Ancient Art, housing a vast collection of paintings, sculptures and drawings from the 15th to the 18th century. The core of the collection is made up of works of art confiscated and deposited by the French State during the revolutionary period.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 4\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWe enjoy an included guided walking tour of Bruges this morning, whose historic city centre is a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Known as the \u2018Venice of the North\u2019, Bruges is famous for lace making, chocolate and its picturesque network of canals. We then spend some time at leisure in Bruges, and on an individual basis you may choose to visit the Groeninge Museum, home to a fine collection of Flemish and Belgian painting from Jan Van Eyck to Marcel Broodthears, a wide range of Renaissance and Baroque Masters and representations of Belgian Symbolism and Modernism. Later in the afternoon, an included 45 minute cruise on the picturesque canals of Bruges enables us to see the city from a different perspective.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 5\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWe visit Ghent this morning for a guided walking tour. Originally the capital of Flanders, Ghent retains more historical buildings than any other Flemish city. Here we visit St Bavo Cathedral, which is home to the famous 24-panel altarpiece \u2018The Adoration of the Mystic Lamb\u2019. This afternoon we have a guided tour at the Ghent Museum of Fine Arts with works ranging from 19th century Realism, Romanticism, Impressionism, Symbolism and Expressionism to 20th century Surrealism including two works by Hieronymus Bosch. A farewell dinner is included at a carefully selected restaurant close to our hotel this evening.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 6\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWe visit Waterloo with our tour manager today. On arrival we visit Memorial 1815 to see the Panorama of the Battle, a canvas dating back to 1912. Created by Louis Dumoulin, a French naval painter, the canvas is one of the largest in the world and offers a panoramic view measuring 110 metres long and 12 metres high. There is then the option of climbing 226 steps to reach the top of the Lions Mound, a monument commemorating the battlefield of Waterloo. We then visit the Wellington Museum, located in an inn dating back to the start of the 18th century. Accommodating the headquarters of the Duke of Wellington, the museum traces the events of the era and perpetuates the memory of the nations and men who participated in the battle. We return to Brussels for our return Eurostar train to London St Pancras in second class coaches. We then continue by coach back to our original pick up points.\u003C/p\u003E\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "alias": "featurePropertyFeatureDescription",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": ""
        },
        {
          "alias": "featurePropertyFeatureSummary",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": {
              "contentData": [],
              "settingsData": [],
              "expose": [],
              "layout": {}
            },
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003ENB:- Please note we reserve the right to alter the above itinerary if required for operational\nreasons. Given the historic nature of the places visited, a certain degree of walking will be involved, and this holiday may not be suitable for those with mobility problems.\u003C/p\u003E\n"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
